{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6f9","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07664-b32a-7b03-be28-978067559ac9"},"sequence":471,"recorded_at":1780531400000937,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"4279c805-073c-4405-a747-7b1a47e438ac","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"4279c805-073c-4405-a747-7b1a47e438ac","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"4279c805-073c-4405-a747-7b1a47e438ac"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"ec10fbce-b518-448f-94a9-b4852705ffc0"},"task_id":"ec10fbce-b518-448f-94a9-b4852705ffc0","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"ec10fbce-b518-448f-94a9-b4852705ffc0","chunk":"Read text file `/tmp/verdict.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-3bkz0c — review verdict, CR rev3 (`story_final`), round 1\n2|\n3|- Verdict: **changes requested** → `to-dev`\n4|- repeat-of: `none` (rev1 and rev2 never reached a reviewer; they failed CR\n5|  validation at command 4/18)\n6|- Reviewer run: RUN-260906-bd76f6\n7|- Candidate: base `1cb6b93` → tree `292bc4242de751821d59ee1e3e68c9f1670b4634`,\n8|  head `1296ecc`, `repository_delta=present`\n9|- Tree OID re-verified `292bc4242…` **after** every probe and mutant below.\n10|\n11|## What holds\n12|\n13|| Claim | Probe I ran | Result |\n14|| --- | --- | ---: |\n15|| candidate == worktree head | `git rev-parse HEAD^{tree}` | `292bc4242…`, clean |\n16|| frozen leaves untouched | `git diff --stat d5ad5f6 1296ecc` | LOGBOOK + `internal/environ` only |\n17|| suite green at head | `go test ./... -count=1` | 19/19 packages ok |\n18|| build / vet / fmt | `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `gofmt -l` | exit 0; `gofmt` flags only gitignored `.temp/` scratch |\n19|| environ coverage | `go test -cover` | 76.1%, reproduced |\n20|| landability, mechanical | `git fetch origin main` | `origin/main == 1cb6b93 == CR base`; HEAD 3 ahead; fast-forward clean, no rebase |\n21|| tuple agreement bidirectional | read `tuple_agreement_test.go` | yes — both judges must accept, or both refuse carrying the rule phrase |\n22|| frame agreement bidirectional | T1 mutant, below | yes — a judge that flips its answer reddens its row |\n23|| T1 token-preserving mutant is real | I re-applied it myself | census stays **green**, **7** behavioural rows redden (producer claimed 5; the two `*_escape_falls_to_syntax` rows also flip). Kill is behavioural, not nominal. |\n24|\n25|## B1 (blocking) — the census detects a known *name*, not a new *copy*\n26|\n27|`census_test.go:24-27` claims: *\"A new copy of a shared rule — a fourth facade\n28|`decodeStrictObject`, **a third surrogate-gate spelling**, **a new string-measure\n29|helper** — fails here as an unregistered site.\"* Two of the three named shapes do\n30|not fire. I control-planted seven shapes into `internal/provider`, an **in-scope**\n31|census package, and ran `TestSharedImplementationsAreCensused` +\n32|`TestSharedGrammarsAreOneLanguage` on each:\n33|\n34|| # | Planted shape (in `internal/provider`) | Caught |\n35|| --- | --- | ---: |\n36|| A | `func decodeStrictObject` — ledgered name | **YES** |\n37|| C3 | method `func (surrogateScanner) hasLoneSurrogateEscape` — ledgered name | **YES** |\n38|| B1 | `func parseStrictObject` — a genuine fourth strict decoder, fresh spelling | **NO** |\n39|| B2 | `func scanForLoneSurrogate` — a third surrogate-gate spelling | **NO** |\n40|| B3 | `func measureString` — a new **byte**-counting string measure | **NO** |\n41|| C1 | `var stringLength = byteLength` — ledgered name, byte measure, `var` binding | **NO** |\n42|| C2 | `var decodeStrict = <alias-bound closure>` — ledgered name, `var` binding | **NO** |\n43|\n44|5 of 7 pass. `scanSharedSymbols` inspects `*ast.FuncDecl` names against a fixed\n45|literal table (`sharedFunctionSymbols`), so anything that is not a func/method\n46|declaration carrying an already-known identifier is invisible. B3 and C1 are the\n47|damaging ones: a **byte**-counting measure — the exact §1.6 drift this Story\n48|exists to prevent — lands in a scanned package and the census stays green.\n49|\n50|Consequence for the AC ledger: boundary.md row 1 (\"One environment library;\n51|census forbids new copies\", named test `TestSharedImplementationsAreCensused`) is\n52|**not driven**. Reported AC coverage is **5 of 7 rows driven**, not 7 of 7 (row 1\n53|fails under control plant; row 7 — see F1; row 6 is a legitimate stated bound).\n54|\n55|What would close it: derive the denominator from *behaviour or shape*, not from a\n56|name table (e.g. reject any in-scope function whose body reaches\n57|`utf8.RuneCount*`/`len()` on a bound member, or any second JSON entry point), or\n58|narrow the claim in the file comment to exactly what the scan can see and state\n59|the rest as an explicit bound. A name registry described as a copy census is the\n60|unsupported-capability shape.\n61|\n62|## B2 (blocking) — the ledgered divergence is **not** over-strictness only\n63|\n64|The CR, the LOGBOOK entry, and `boundary.md` all characterise the recorded\n65|divergence one way: raw-scan facades **refuse**, string-walk judges **accept**.\n66|G-B asked whether there is a direction where a facade **admits** what another\n67|refuses. There is.\n68|\n69|Witness — one wire value, `\"\\\\ud800\\udc00\"`: an escaped backslash, the **literal\n70|text** `ud800`, then a **real** `\\udc00` escape, i.e. a lone LOW surrogate.\n71|\n72|| Judge | Production entry | Verdict |\n73|| --- | --- | ---: |\n74|| environ | `DecodeStrictObject` | refuse — `lone surrogate escape` |\n75|| canonicaljson | `Canonicalize` | refuse |\n76|| provhost | `DecodeManifest` | refuse — `lone surrogate escape` |\n77|| **sessadapter** | `DecodeTuple` | **ADMIT** — `Version = \"\\\\ud800\\uFFFD\"` |\n78|| **dirnode** | `CheckScanRequest` | **ADMIT** |\n79|\n80|Mechanism: the raw scan skips the first byte of the `\\\\` pair, then reads the\n81|literal text `ud800` at the *second* backslash as a high-surrogate escape and\n82|pairs it with the following real `\\udc00`. Both frozen facades therefore admit a\n83|body whose decoded value `encoding/json` has silently rewritten to U+FFFD — the\n84|exact harm `internal/environ/decode.go:104-108` names as the gate's reason to\n85|exist. Found by differential fuzz (400k vectors, seed 20260906) against\n86|`HasLoneSurrogateEscape`, then reduced to the deterministic witness above.\n87|\n88|Why the battery is green over it: `frameCorpus()` samples backslash-run parity\n89|(run 1 / 2 / 3) but never composes an **even run with a following real escape**.\n90|`escaped backslash high run2` is `A\\\\ud800B` — literal text, nothing after it. The\n91|class was sampled on the over-strict side only and the safe conclusion was\n92|inferred from that sample. That is the \"property inferred from an incomplete\n93|sample, reported as established\" shape, and it is the specific defect this leaf\n94|was created to catch — leaf 1's one-directional\n95|`TestSurrogateGateAgreesWithCanonicalJSON` is named in the brief for hiding\n96|exactly this class, and the replacement battery hides the more dangerous half of it.\n97|\n98|To close: add the composed shape to `frameCorpus()` with the real per-judge\n99|verdicts, correct the ledger text in `census_test.go`, `doc.go`, `boundary.md`\n100|and the LOGBOOK entry to say the divergence is **bidirectional and includes an\n101|admission hole**, and — B2b — record the frozen-scanner unification as a **board\n102|element**. It currently exists only as prose (\"Genuinely-required frozen-package\n103|change (not made)\") in `boundary.md` and the LOGBOOK; there is no board item for\n104|it. The freeze is a legitimate reason not to fix the scanners in this leaf; it is\n105|not a reason to ledger the class as harmless.\n106|\n107|## F1 (must fix, non-blocking on its own) — the library is behind nothing, and says otherwise\n108|\n109|No file outside `internal/environ` imports it. The only occurrence of the import\n110|path anywhere else in the tree is inside an error string in `census_test.go:241`.\n111|Six production functions sit at **0.0%** statement coverage — no production\n112|caller and no test:\n113|\n114|| Symbol | Callers in tree |\n115|| --- | ---: |\n116|| `CheckUint53Bounds` (exported) | 0 |\n117|| `CheckSortedUniqueStrings` (exported) | 0 |\n118|| `rawUint53`, `parseUint53Literal` | reachable only via the above |\n119|| `Fault.Error()` (exported) | 0 |\n120|| `validCapability` | **0 anywhere** — dead, incl. inside its own package |\n121|\n122|That is most of the 23.9% coverage gap. Two specifics:\n123|\n124|- `CheckSortedUniqueStrings`'s doc comment states *\"an unsorted-but-unique vector\n125|  and a sorted-but-duplicated vector are separate rows in the observation\n126|  battery, so a check that enforced only one half could not pass both.\"* Those\n127|  rows exist for `CheckSortedUniqueDigests`, not for this function. The comment\n128|  asserts coverage the function does not have.\n129|- `validCapability`'s comment claims *\"It is a table loop so the census derives it\n130|  like every other closed vocabulary\"* — it is in no census table and is called\n131|  from nowhere.\n132|\n133|Also: boundary.md row 1 names `environ.DecodeStrictObject` as the \"production\n134|call site\". It has no production caller. Wiring the facades onto `environ` was\n135|correctly out of scope under the freeze — so say that as a stated bound rather\n136|than presenting an uncalled function as a production call site. Delete or drive\n137|the dead exports; a library that advertises API nothing reaches is the AC-row-7\n138|shape (\"no unsupported capability advertised\") turned inward.\n139|\n140|## F2 (finding) — the mutation ratio is killed-over-applied on a self-chosen denominator\n141|\n142|Reported: \"Applied 7, killed 7. Narrowing kills 6 of 6… Survivors: none. Every\n143|surviving-mutant bound is therefore vacuous; no bound needs stating.\"\n144|\n145|Re-derived from `internal/environ` production source: **31** `refuse(...)` arms\n146|(`decode.go` 1, `tuple.go` 10, `observation.go` 21) and **58** boolean gate exits\n147|returning `false` — **89 refusal exits**. Six narrowing mutants is **6 of 89\n148|(6.7%)**, not 6 of 6. For scale, the two sibling leaves ran 144 and 185 mutants\n149|against comparable surfaces. The DoD line \"Every gate ships at least one NARROWING\n150|mutant\" is not met, and \"no bound needs stating\" is the inverted claim: the 83\n151|unattacked exits *are* the bound and must be stated as one.\n152|\n153|Untouched whole classes include every `checkCapabilityResult` arm except the\n154|`!=`→`<` length check (N4), both `CheckSortedUnique*` orderings, every\n155|`Check{Digest,UUIDv7,Timestamp}` bridge, the `rawUint53` magnitude ladder, and the\n156|reason-coherence pair at `observation.go:359-364`.\n157|\n158|## Notes, reported as unknown rather than inferred\n159|\n160|- **rev1/rev2 command 4/18.** Not recoverable from the attached artifacts: the\n161|  harness caps the log at 64 KiB and drops 4.73 MB **from the middle** — exactly\n162|  where the failure was. The producer's root cause (a `canonicaljson`\n163|  wall-clock flake, `TestTransferManifestMaximumEntryGateIsLinear`) is consistent\n164|  with its own `reverify.md`, but I could not confirm it from the artifact. I can\n165|  confirm the suite is green at the committed head; I cannot confirm what was red\n166|  in rev1/rev2. Worth raising against the CR harness separately — a validation log\n167|  that truncates its own failure is not evidence.\n168|- `GOOS=windows go vet` and `-race` are producer claims; I did not rerun them.\n169|\n170|## Story landability (`story_final`)\n171|\n172|**Mechanically:** yes. `origin/main` is still `1cb6b93` — identical to the CR base\n173|— HEAD is 3 commits ahead, fast-forward clean with no rebase, all 19 packages\n174|green, `go build`/`go vet` exit 0. Nothing here would redden `main` on first push.\n175|\n176|**On merit: no.** B2 means the Story would land two protocol hosts that admit a\n177|lone low surrogate on their wire and hand the caller bytes `encoding/json`\n178|silently rewrote — while its own final leaf, whose entire purpose is to catch that\n179|class, ledgers it as harmless over-strictness and ships a green battery\n180|asserting the measurement was made. Landing it puts a live admission hole into\n181|`main` **under a boundary that claims to have checked for it**, which is worse\n182|than landing it under no boundary at all. Close B1 and B2 before integration."}}}
